// cave_arrival_baked.h — D.3 baked cave-arrival table (overworld *_exit block +
// world flags per cave interior), captured via the walkabout/--dump-cave-doors
// tooling. Preloaded by the decoupled runtime so every door is one-way from the
// start. Regenerate by re-running the capture walkabout and copying
// cave_arrival_capture.txt over this file.
// #included mid-rando.c after the RandoCaveArrival typedef (it references it).
//
// KEYED BY interior_id, NOT BY POSITION. It used to be a bare positional array
// loaded with g_cave_capture[i] = kCaveArrivalBaked[i], which silently
// misaligned the moment an interior was inserted mid-list: splitting
// refill_cave_1 and fairy_cave_5 in two shifted every later row onto the wrong
// door, so eight interiors emerged at another cave's overworld position in
// decoupled mode (and the last one had no data at all). Name keying makes that
// class of drift impossible — an unmatched name is reported, not absorbed.
//
// Each row below was re-attributed to its interior by matching the captured
// block's own overworld area against that interior's door rows, so the
// attribution is derived from the data rather than from list order.
//
// Coverage: 37 of the 46 pool entries. The nine without a row load with
// valid == 0, which the runtime already degrades to the coupled exit — emerge at
// the door you entered. Never a softlock; a fidelity gap in decoupled mode only,
// closed by a capture walkabout (ZELDA3_CAPTURE_ARRIVALS=1) over those doors:
//   general_store_2, general_store_3, refill_cave_1_graveyard,
//   fairy_cave_5_checkerboard, general_store_2_dark_sanctuary,
//   general_store_2_dw_death_mountain, general_store_3_outcasts,
//   general_store_3_potion, kakariko_lame_shop_lumberjack_house
//
// Eight of those nine are simply the extra doors of multi-door rooms that the
// original walkabout never visited separately (a capture is keyed by the door
// ENTERED, so one visit fills one door).
//
// The ninth, general_store_2_dw_death_mountain, was DROPPED rather than kept: its
// captured world flags said LIGHT (is_dark 0x00 / save_dark 0x00) while the same
// block's own overworld_area_index_exit is 0x0045 — bit 0x40 set, i.e. dark
// screen 0x05. Every other dark row carries (0x01, 0x40) and every light row
// (0x00, 0x00), 37 for 37, so this was a bad capture, not a convention. Replaying
// it would have written is_in_dark_world = 0 AND the SRAM-persisted
// savegame_is_darkworld = 0 while standing on Dark World Death Mountain, which
// mirror/bunny gating and the overworld map all key off. Honest-but-degraded
// beats confidently-wrong; re-capture it on the walkabout. The self-check now
// asserts area-vs-flags agreement so a row like it cannot return unnoticed.
//
// embedded-data-guard: allow — this is vanilla overworld *positional* data (Link
// x/y + camera + scroll-window per cave door), captured from the running game
// (which itself runs off the ROM-extracted zelda3_assets.dat), NOT a creative ROM
// asset. It cannot be reconstructed from kExitData_* (caves take the cached
// entry-snapshot branch, overworld.c LoadOverworldFromDungeon, and are absent from
// the exit-data table); a clean offline derivation requires reproducing the
// overworld camera/scroll-centering math from the entrance-door tables. That
// runtime-derivation (to drop this committed copy) is a tracked follow-up; until
// then this table is a conscious, documented exception.
typedef struct { const char *interior_id; RandoCaveArrival arrival; } RandoCaveArrivalBaked;
